description.narrative:
Action Against Hunger (AAH) is a global humanitarian organisation that takes action against the causes and effects of hunger and malnutrition. AAH international network (AAH-IN) is composed of AAH entities from France, (Sida agreement partner), AAH India, AAH United Kingdom, AAH Spain, AAH USA and AAH Canada. Each of these members are separate legal entities, but share a common charter of principles, strategy and areas of intervention. All members of the network are committed to bringing coherence and enhancing efficiency to better serve those in need of nourishment , and to adhere to the AAH principles for which it stands. These principles are: moral and financial independence, strict political and religious neutrality, non-discrimination, free and direct access to victims, professionalism and transparency. The principles are outlined in an agreement (the International Global Protocols), which is signed by all AAH members.

Through this contribution, Sida will be supporting AAH with funding which will allow for AAH to provide humanitarian assistance to affected population in more than 20 countries worldwide, such as Syria, Burkina Faso, South Sudan and Ukraine. While the specific objective of each intervention might differ, AAH's main mandate is within the nutrition sector, with the mission to "save, improve and protect lives by eliminating hunger through the prevention, detection and treatment of undernutrition, especially during and after emergency crises caused by situations of conflict, displacement, poverty, discrimination, inequality, or natural disaster". Nutrition interventions are complemented by activities which seek to ensure that people have access to clean water, food, healthcare and protection. By being present before and after disaster strikes, AAH is committed not only to respond to emergency needs, but also to build and to strengthen the capacity and resilience of the local communities. In line with the Sida humanitarian strategy, the objective of the response is to save lives, alleviate suffering and uphold human dignity for the benefit of people in need who have been, or are at risk of becoming, affected by armed conflicts, natural disasters or other disaster emergencies.

AAH's long-term planning and performance monitoring process is laid out in a five-year International Strategic Plan (ISP), which is prepared by the AAH international network. The current International Strategic Plan (ISP3) covers the period 2021-2025 and reflects AAH's priorities. To meet the overarching impact of collective and inclusive action that empowers 25 million people to overcome hunger in 2025, the key priorities to be addressed by AAH are to:
1) Save lives: Lives are saved and immediate needs are met in an effective and inclusive manner in emergency contexts;
2) Collectively build resilience: People and communities are better able to withstand future shocks. Mitigation of the climate crisis and of gender inequality 
is embedded in the fight against hunger;
3) Connect and mobilize: Citizens, communities, civil society and actors at all levels are mobilized as part of a collective movement to end hunger; 
4) Create and share knowledge: Best practice in the fight against hunger is developed collaboratively by a diversity of actors and shared for the empowerment of all.

In 2022, Sida supports AAH's humanitarian interventions in 20 specific contexts, as well as supporting AAH's capacity to implement life-saving responses
through the Rapid Response Mechanism (RRM) and lastly, with method and capacity building support.

Child Protection

•	Construct 3 new Child Friendly Spaces (CFS) with WASH facilities and support 5 existing CFS.
•	Provide recreational activities for 5600 children (52% girls).  
•	Train 40 community animators on Gender Based Violence (GBV) awareness and peace building (60% women).
•	Form 3  new and improve 5 existing Child Rights Clubs (children) and train 240 girls and boys on child rights (50% girls);
•	Referral systems developed in the 3 new payams (Gakdong, Denjok and Likuangole) and improving referral systems in 2 existing payams;
•	Identify separated and unaccompanied children (girls and boys) and support/refer to child protection facility (actual numbers unknown, but at least 80% of the identified children to be supported);
•	Identify and form 3 new functioning child protection committees (adults), improve 5 existing committees (30 per committee at least 50% female) 
•	Procure and distribute 3 sets of recreational materials for the new Child Friendly Spaces, and replenish materials for existing 5 CFS

WASH

•	Drill and install one (1) solar motorized water system in Pibor centre reaching 750 beneficiaries.
•	Rehabilitate 15 existing boreholes, benefitting approximately 5,000 people 
•	Test and analyse water quality of 15 rehabilitated boreholes and one solar motorized water system.
•	Train 45 new hand pump mechanics and refresher training for 120 existing hand pump mechanics (15% female); 
•	Establish water management committees (WMC) around 15 rehabilitated boreholes and support existing 37 WMCs (6 person/ committee, 50% female);  
•	Train 60 community hygiene promoters in 3 new payams on basic hygiene promotion methodologies and simple messaging and refresher training for existing 74 hygiene promoters (70% female)
•	Provide hand pump repair tools and spare parts for 15 rehabilitated boreholes and one solar motorized system 
•	Distribute hygiene kits to 2,000  vulnerable women and girls from the IDPs and host communities;

Civil Rights Defenders (CRD)  presented a proposal for a 3-year and 3-month programme to Sida in June 2020. The overall objective of the Project is to deter the pervasive impunity in South Sudan and promote a peaceful and inclusive development, as envisioned in Agenda 2020 - SDG 16, through ensuring accountability for human rights violation. The proposal was a result of a Sida-funded preparation phase July 2019-February 2020. 

The Project comprises 4 components:

1. Organisational Assessments and Financial and Administrative Capacity Strengthening of 15 CSOs in total throughout the project period;

2. Defending the Defenders, Security Interventions - physical and psychosocial security training, a digital Training of Trainer programme, expansion of the Natalia Project (a protection project), and emergency grants to HRDs;

3. Fighting Impunity through Legal Accountability - implementation of the Defenders Database, grants to CSOs who are successfully taking part in the defenders Database training, legal action sub-granting and capacity support to HRDs and lawyers, networking with regional and international organisations;

4. Accountability Advocacy and Networks Expansion - advocacy visits to the African Court of Human Rights and to the EU, participation of HRDs at Defenders Day, joint campaigns and publication of reports.

The total budget of the project is SEK 23,710,000. 5,5 MSEK (23% of the total budget) will be transferred to local partners mainly in the form of grants to build local capacity.

Civil Rights Defenders presenterade ett slutligt förlag till Sida i juni 2020 om ett projekt på 3 år och 3 månader. Projektets övergripande syfte är att avskräcka från den rådande straffriheten i Sydsudan och främja en fredlig och inkluderande utveckling, i samstämmighet med Agenda 2030 - SDG 16, genom  att säkerställa ansvarsutkrävande för människorättsbrott.

Projektet består av fyra komponenter:

1. Organisationsbedömning och finansiellt och administrativt kapacitetsstärkande av totalt 15 sydsudanska civilsamhällesorganisationer ;

2. Försvara männiksorättsförsvararna, säkerhetsinterventioner - fysisk och psykosocial säkerhetsutbildning, ett digitalt Training-of-Trainer-program, utvidgning av Natalia-projektet (ett projekt för skydd) och bidrag till människorättsförsvarare vid krissituationer (emergency grants);

3. Bekämpa straffrihet genom rättsliga åtgärder (legal accountability) - användande av Defenders Database, bidrag till civilsamhällesorganisationer som framgångsrikt deltagit i utbildning i databasen, bidrag till rättsligt arbete och kapacitetsutveckling av människorättsförsvarare och advokater, nätverkande med regionala och internationella organisationer;

4. Påverkansarbete och utvidgning av nätverk - påverkan gentemot Afrikanska MR-domstolen och EU, deltagande av männiksorättsförsvarare vid internationella Defenders Day, gemensamma kampanjer och publicering av rapporter.

Insatsens totala budget är SEK 23 710 000. 5,5 MSEK (23 % av den totala budgeten) kommer att vidarebefordras till nationella organisationer och aktörer i Sydsudan i huvudsak i form av bidrag för kapacitetsstärkande åtgärder.

The project has to contribute to the following impact and must achieve the outcomes and outputs as stated in your grant application:

Long-term impact for Food and Nutrition Security:

1. Reduce malnutrition
2. Promote agricultural growth
3. Ecologically sustainable food systems

Long-term impact for Security and Rule of Law:

1. Social and Economic Reconstruction

Medium-term impact that will contribute to this:

(I) Education system (TVET/HE) is of good quality, relevant and accessible (SDG 4);

(II) Partnerships between persons and organisations are inclusive and sustainable (SDG 17);

(III) Organisations key to (sectoral) inclusive development of partner countries are strengthened by inflow of enhanced workforce.

Outcomes:

TVET/HE organisations (in the selected partner countries and in NL) perform better their core tasks, firmly embedded in their environment (in line with country / regional specific labour market needs & aiming at inclusiveness).

To that end, the following ++outputs++ stated in your grant application must be achieved by the end of the project:

1. Curricula of Joint Regional Master and Short Courses designed;
2. Joined Regional Master Programme (JRM) and Short Courses (SCs) delivered;
3. Pool of qualified HE/TVET instructors created;
4. Living Lab Platform established;
5. Regional Hubs of Knowledge and Capacity formed;
6. Long-term N-S and S-S partnership established.
